[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The one tolerated difference: NaN payload bits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `eq_f32` in the test harness is bit-exact — `+0.0 != -0.0`, `+inf != -inf`, NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never equals a number — with a single tolerance: **two NaNs compare equal even
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if their payload/sign bits differ.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Root cause: gcc `-O0` and LLVM choose *opposite* SSE operand orders for the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C expression. For `a.x += b.x` gcc emits `addss %xmm1,%xmm0` with `b.x` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `xmm0` (computing `b + a`, so `b` is `src1`); LLVM emits `addps %xmm1,%xmm0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (computing `a + b`, so `a` is `src1`). x86 returns `src1` when **both** operands
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are NaN, so the two builds propagate a different input NaN. It also shows up
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with internally generated NaNs: `0.0 * inf` raises invalid-operation and yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the x86 "QNaN indefinite" `0xffc00000`, which then meets a propagated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0x7fc00000` in the following add inside `c2Dot` / `c2Det2` / `c2Mulrv`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m It is not fixable from Rust source: LLVM canonicalizes commutative `fadd`/`fmul`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operand order and *merges* `b + a` with `a + b` into a single function — verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by compiling both spellings and observing `nm` report two names at one address.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Matching gcc `-O0` would need per-expression inline assembly, and would then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stop matching a C build at any other optimization level.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m It is also unobservable in anything the library decides: every `<` / `>` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/src/lib.c` is false when either side is NaN, whatever the payload.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/nan_payload.rs` asserts this directly — with arbitrary NaN payloads and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signaling NaNs on the input, **every `int` result** (including `omni_collide`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m across all 9 type pairs × 30 000 iterations) and every non-NaN float result is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bit-identical, and `is_nan()` agreement is exact.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
